Q: Odd effect with local package with mod_perl and registry A: <p>Move constants to the top, <strong>before</strong> any inline package declarations, and explicitly qualify them with <code>our</code>:</p>
<pre><code># At the top of your script
use constant XYZ =&gt; 'some_value';
use constant ABC =&gt; 42;
</code></pre>
<p>Or, if the inline package needs access, <strong>explicitly place the constant in a shared namespace</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>use constant {
    XYZ =&gt; 'value',
    ABC =&gt; 42,
};

{
    package MyInlinePackage;
    # Reference the constant via the registry package or a fixed namespace
    sub do_something {
        return Apache::ROOT::path::to::your_script::XYZ();  # ugly but works
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>A cleaner solution is to <strong>avoid inline packages entirely</strong> and move them to real <code>.pm</code> modules.</p>

Q: prepared statement SELECT gives Call to a member function fetch_assoc() on a non-object A: <p><a href="http://docs.php.net/manual/en/mysqli-stmt.execute.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mysqli_stmt::execute()</a> returns a bool (true/false), not a <a href="http://docs.php.net/manual/en/class.mysqli-result.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mysqli_result</a>.<br />
Since php 5.3 you <em>can</em> use <a href="http://docs.php.net/manual/en/mysqli-stmt.get-result.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mysqli_stmt::get_result</a> to get the mysqli_result from a statement instance.</p>
<pre><code>$stmt = $link-&gt;prepare(&quot;SELECT * FROM people WHERE peopleID = ?&quot;);

$stmt-&gt;bind_param('i', $_GET['peopleID']);

$stmt-&gt;execute();

$result = $stmt-&gt;get_result();
</code></pre>
